            <section class="mt-content-container">
                <section class="box-objectives">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chemistry_1e_(OpenSTAX)/14:_Acid-Base_Equilibria/14.03:_Relative_Strengths_of_Acids_and_Bases" class="mt-section"><span id="Learning_Objectives"></span><h5 class="box-legend lt-chem-38278 editable"><span class="lt-icon-default"><span style="display:none;">&nbsp;</span>Learning Objectives</span></h5>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-38278">Assess the relative strengths of acids and bases according to their ionization constants</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-38278">Rationalize trends in acid&ndash;base strength in relation to molecular structure</li>
    <li class="lt-chem-38278">Carry out equilibrium calculations for weak acid&ndash;base systems</li>
</ul>
</div></section>

<p class="lt-chem-38278">We can rank the strengths of acids by the extent to which they ionize in aqueous solution. The reaction of an acid with water is given by the general expression:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-38278">\[\ce{HA}(aq)+\ce{H2O}(l)&#8652;\ce{H3O+}(aq)+\ce{A-}(aq) \nonumber \]</p>

<p class="lt-chem-38278">Water is the base that reacts with the acid \(\ce{HA}\), \(\ce{A^{&minus;}}\) is the conjugate base of the acid \(\ce{HA}\), and the hydronium ion is the conjugate acid of water. A strong acid yields 100% (or very nearly so) of \(\ce{H3O+}\) and \(\ce{A^{&minus;}}\) when the acid ionizes in water; Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\) lists several strong acids. A weak acid gives small amounts of \(\ce{H3O+}\) and \(\ce{A^{&minus;}}\).</p>

